From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] explorer: a Linux app's window on the taskbar is its app's

A Linux window's button had the program "\sg-linux\<WM_CLASS>": the app
pinned from Start showed twice, a pin and a running button (pins matched
by their shortcut's target, and every Linux app's shortcut starts
sg-linuxapp64.exe); its menu named the class, its Pin to taskbar made a
shortcut to a path that does not exist, its first entry and middle-click
started nothing; no jump list (David 2026-11-27: Linux programs first
class).

As Windows knows a window's app by its AppUserModelID: sg-shell's
sg-linuxapp gives each Linux app's Start shortcut the ID
SG.LinuxApp.<desktop id>, maps the app's window classes to it
(HKCU\Software\Stained Glass\Linux apps\Window classes) and registers it
(HKCU\Software\Classes\AppUserModelId\<ID>: DisplayName,
RelaunchCommand, SgShortcut); its .desktop actions are the ID's jump
list. The taskbar now
- gives a Linux window (framed or stand-in) its class's ID, so it groups
  and takes the app's jump list as a Windows program's window does;
- reads a pinned shortcut's own System.AppUserModel.ID and matches pins
  with windows by it (else by program, as before): the pin is the running
  app's button, on the main and the other displays' taskbars, and a pin's
  menu shows its app's jump list;
- in a Linux window's menu names the app, starts another (its
  RelaunchCommand, also middle-click) and pins the app's own shortcut (a
  copy, pins_add_link); a Linux window that is no app's keeps its menu.

Gate: test/linuxappid-gate.sh (mutants SG_MUTANT_LINUX_NO_APPID,
SG_MUTANT_PIN_NO_APPID).
